Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.400
We're going to talk about sudoku&nbsp;
and in particular something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.600
called the Phistomefel Ring.
- (Brady: Phistomefel ring?)&nbsp;

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:11.960
Well it's named after a German sudoku constructor&nbsp;
called Phistomefel who discovered it a couple of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:17.680
years ago and it's a property that exists&nbsp;
in all sudoku puzzles but not many people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:22.320
know about it and it's it's got a quite a&nbsp;
lovely proof and it might blow your mind.&nbsp;

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:26.720
- (It might, all right. Is it)&nbsp;
(use- like is this like a a secret&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:30.400
that will help people solve sudokus?)
- It can definitely help help you solve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:36.640
some sudokus and yeah maybe- shall I show you?&nbsp;
So maybe while I'm doing this I'll tell you-&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:41.120
I'm sure everybody watching Numberphile knows&nbsp;
the rules of sudoku; the idea of sudoku is you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:49.319
have to put the numbers 1 to 9 just once each&nbsp;
into every row, every column, and into every 3x3 box.

00:00:51.742 --> 00:00:57.000
That is our sudoku. And what I'm going to show&nbsp;
you now is something called set equivalence theory.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.600
So we know that in this sudoku, if we complete&nbsp;
it correctly, there'll be the numbers 1 to 9&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:09.840
once each in every row, column, and box. So I'm now&nbsp;
going to highlight some cells in red: so I'm going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:19.320
to highlight row 3, row 7, this box this&nbsp;
whole box here, and box 6. And the interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:24.160
thing about those those four things I just&nbsp;
highlighted is because of the properties of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:28.800
sudoku we actually know- we don't know what the&nbsp;
disposition of the digits will be within those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:34.840
red cells but we do know exactly what those&nbsp;
red cells contain altogether. Because it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:41.720
four complete sets of the digits 1 to 9; one for&nbsp;
this row, one for this row, one for this box, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:47.240
one for this box. So altogether we know that the&nbsp;
red cells here contain four sets of the digits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:53.920
1 to 9. Now I'm going to switch to green and&nbsp;
I'm going to highlight a different four sets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:57.960
of the digits 1 to 0. So I'm going to highlight&nbsp;
the whole - oh can we see that that's green? So I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:03.360
going to highlight the whole of column 1, the&nbsp;
whole of column 2, the whole of column 8 and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:10.160
whole of column 9. Okay so now let's describe&nbsp;
the the the green set of digits, that's also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:16.760
four sets of the digits 1 to 9; one for each of&nbsp;
the columns. So at this point we know that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:22.080
set of the green cells if you like, and the set&nbsp;
of the red cells, are identical - each equal to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:27.560
four sets of the digits 1 to 9. Now the trick to&nbsp;
understanding the Phistomefel ring is to focus on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:33.360
one of these cells that has two colours in it. So&nbsp;
let's look at this cell here and imagine that we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:41.760
removed it from both the green set and the red&nbsp;
set. So let's let's imagine that we we just take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:49.560
this out - I'll make it blue. So I'm removing this&nbsp;
from both the green set and the red set. What can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:56.080
we say about the remaining cells now in green&nbsp;
and red? And the thing we can say is that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:00.680
still contain the same set of digits. We don't&nbsp;
know now exactly what that set of digits is,&nbsp;

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:06.400
because we don't know what digit will fill in&nbsp;
row 3 column 1 in the final solution, but we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:12.640
do know that the red set and the green set are&nbsp;
still equivalent, they're still equal. And we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:19.240
do that for every single- every single cell in&nbsp;
this puzzle that contains both colours. We can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:27.320
remove it from both the green set and the red set.&nbsp;
Let's now focus on what we've got left now. So we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:31.840
know at this point that the green and the red&nbsp;
set sets are still equivalent, and if we look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:39.040
the red set you can see 16 cells here ringing&nbsp;
the central box of the sudoku. Now this is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:48.240
Phistomefel ring, and we know that that is exactly&nbsp;
the same digits as appear in the four corners of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:54.240
the sudoku - these four 2x2s in the corners. And&nbsp;
you can try this on any sudoku you solve on your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.240 --> 00:04:02.599
train home, fill it in and then ring this central&nbsp;
box and compare those digits to the corners 2x2s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.599 --> 00:04:08.595
and you will find that this works. It works&nbsp;
for every sudoku and it's- I think it's quite magical.

00:04:20.612 --> 00:04:25.000
(One of the questions that pops into&nbsp;
my head is, could you make any other shapes or)&nbsp;

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:31.240
(configurations on a grid that have this property?)
- Brady, you are brilliant. Yes, you can indeed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:38.520
um and in fact there's a famous Dutch constructor&nbsp;
called Aad van de Wetering who- he found another trick.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:45.360
He found that there's a square in the top left&nbsp;
of a sudoku and a square in the bottom right of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:53.040
the sudoku that have a relationship using exactly&nbsp;
this trick. And we've even seen puzzles now where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:59.000
you can use this feature to create the difference&nbsp;
of squares equation in sudoku form - it sounds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:07.080
completely crazy but you can see it on the screen&nbsp;
here. And you can create some mathematics out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:13.960
the geometry of sudoku that actually can yield&nbsp;
to some really beautiful results. So- and it's very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:19.640
very much in in sort of really advanced sudoku&nbsp;
solving, this equivalence of different parts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:26.126
the geometry of the grid is important and can be-&nbsp;
it can crack some fiendish puzzles. 

